# Verified command execution implementation plan

Source proposal: `docs/verified-command-execution-proposal.md`

Prerequisite plan: `docs/directed-acyclic-graph.todo`

Related contracts:

- `docs/test-runner-service-design.md`
- `docs/new-command-proposals.md`

## Goal

Replace every built-in and project-defined public command path with one
run-scoped, Cypress-like verified-command runner. Every invocation must have a
finite wall-clock deadline, an explicit read-only preflight gate, one declared
primary-execution policy, truthful intrinsic verification, optional caller
verification, stage-aware evidence, and effect-driven verified cleanup. The
same architecture must support suite-, test-, and service-owned cleanup,
run-wide resource claims, explicit dependency ordering, retained transaction
history, workflows, and proof-gated execution retry.

Preserve existing public call shapes and return values where the proposal
requires compatibility, but perform a complete definition-based conversion:
do not add a runtime adapter for callback-only project commands or retain a
parallel command engine after migration.

## Plan-wide execution rules

- [ ] Treat `docs/verified-command-execution-proposal.md` as authoritative for
      command execution, timeout placement, retry, cleanup transactions,
      resource ownership, protocol evidence, migration, and validation cadence.
- [ ] Treat `docs/directed-acyclic-graph-proposal.md` and its separate `.todo`
      as the complete prerequisite graph contract; do not duplicate or extend
      that utility in this plan.
- [ ] Treat `docs/new-command-proposals.md` as authoritative only for managed
      fixture domain semantics; resolve execution-model conflicts in favor of
      the verified-command proposal.
- [ ] Treat `docs/test-runner-service-design.md` as authoritative for admission,
      leases, quarantine, retention, acknowledgement, cursor reads, persistence
      ownership, and the presentation-neutral UI boundary.
- [ ] Keep every new task unchecked until its implementation, proportionate
      evidence, and required follow-up review are complete.
- [ ] Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash or commit
      user work.
- [ ] Add language-standard documentation comments for every introduced or
      changed class and method.
- [ ] Keep driver modules independent of `dwarfspec.host`; inject narrow
      capabilities through run assembly.
- [ ] Preserve Busted ownership of test structure, assertions, and behavior
      result classification.
- [ ] Preserve the proposal's non-goal boundaries.
  - [ ] Do not introduce a JavaScript-style command queue, Promise model, DOM
        semantics, fluent-chain retry engine, or a replacement assertion
        framework.
  - [ ] Do not infer product-specific intent from generic input or treat a
        completed render as proof of correct rendered content.
  - [ ] Do not claim arbitrary synchronous Lua/native code can be interrupted;
        require bounded cooperative command and cleanup callbacks.
  - [ ] Do not redesign controller discovery, transport, result persistence,
        TestBed, or the host service beyond the evidence and lifecycle changes
        required by the proposal.
- [ ] Apply the proposal's risk-based validation cadence.
  - [ ] Use syntax/static checks, the relevant conformance case, and a focused
        command suite for a mechanical definition conversion.
  - [ ] Add the smallest relevant live case only for command-specific native
        semantic changes.
  - [ ] Run the complete command-engine suite after runner, deadline, outcome,
        cleanup, or workflow-engine changes.
  - [ ] Run focused protocol, journal, persistence, configuration, host-loading,
        declaration, and formatting checks after schema changes.
  - [ ] Run the full unit suite and representative live qualification at each
        completed command-family boundary, not after each mechanical command.
  - [ ] Reserve full source/package/framework/live qualification for final
        consolidation and other explicitly identified integration gates.

