## Phase 5: Implement run-wide resource claim policy

Outcome: one `ResourceDependencyIndex` validates inert plans and tracks every
active effect-backed claim across all execution-owner levels.

- [x] Add the run-scoped `ResourceDependencyIndex` over one qualified
      `DirectedAcyclicGraph` instance.
- [x] Implement stable run-unique active claim IDs and immutable
      `ResourceClaimReference` values with minimum owner/resource correlation.
- [x] Expose claim references only through an owning transaction or a stable
      resource-producing command result whose documented domain contract
      explicitly permits downstream dependency or compatible sharing.
- [x] Reject forged, stale, foreign-run, or policy-incompatible references.
- [x] Implement inert `ResourceClaimPlanEntry` validation.
  - [x] Require a nonempty invocation-local unique `claim_key`.
  - [x] Support exact existing-resource and provisional creation identities.
  - [x] Derive provisional creation identity from invocation ID and additionally
        the stable operation key for `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE`.
  - [x] Keep local relationship keys distinct from references to active claims.
  - [x] Validate complete plans without allocating claim IDs, creating a
        cleanup transaction, publishing events, or changing index state.
- [x] Implement `ResourceClaimBinding` validation against a previously frozen
      plan.
  - [x] Permit activation of an explicit subset by `claim_key`.
  - [x] Bind provisional entries to concrete stable identities from the effect
        receipt.
  - [x] Reject duplicate/unknown keys, incompatible identities, new
        relationships, and unplanned pre-existing exclusive resources.
- [x] Implement exact post-effect `ResourceClaimRegistration` validation for
      downstream cleanup registration without provisional identities.
- [x] Enforce run-wide exclusive conflicts across service, suite, test, and
      command lifetimes rather than registry-local conflicts.
- [x] Support only explicitly declared compatible sharing, dependency,
      consumption, and transfer contracts.
- [x] Default consumption to the claim's exact owner unless an explicit command
      contract authorizes sharing, dependency, consumption, or transfer.
- [x] Enforce parent owner chains and lifetime direction.
  - [x] Treat command lifetime as nested within its suite/test owner.
  - [x] Treat test-attempt lifetime as nested within suite execution.
  - [x] Treat suite-execution lifetime as nested within service run.
  - [x] Reject sibling or incomparable dependencies unless an explicit transfer
        command has already rehomed ownership.
- [x] Insert prerequisite-to-dependent claim edges only after all DwarfSpec
      policy checks pass.
- [x] Prevent releasing a prerequisite while any active dependent remains.
- [x] Implement terminal claim-disposition policy through transaction-owned
      atomic index operations.
  - [x] Release every claim only after the transaction's restoration and
        required verification succeed.
  - [x] Release `abandoned` transaction claims only through Phase 7's
        runner-only `abandonSelfRolledBack` transition after its typed absence
        proof succeeds.
  - [x] Make every released `ResourceClaimReference` stale immediately.
  - [x] Retain claims after `failed` or `unconfirmed` cleanup as fail-closed
        ownership and quarantine evidence, including `dependency_blocked`
        failures that did not execute restoration.
  - [x] Reject a `complete` disposition if atomic claim release fails; preserve
        the transaction and claims under a composed failed/quarantined outcome.
- [x] Keep fail-closed conflicted claim records as quarantine evidence without
      treating them as normal active graph claims.
- [x] Keep runtime claim/index state separate from authoritative cleanup
      history and result projection.
- [x] Add exhaustive focused tests for plan purity, reference validation,
      binding, conflicts, compatible sharing, lifetime direction, cross-level
      lookup, dependency edges, verified complete release, release blocking,
      stale references, failed/unconfirmed retention, and fail-closed conflict
      evidence.
- [x] Treat every attempt plan as the conservative possible claim set of one
      prospective transaction even when the effect may bind only a subset; use
      separate workflow steps when this conservative simulation would reject
      otherwise independent effects.
- [x] Perform a follow-up review against the proposal's complete claim contract.

Completion criteria:

- [x] No active claim can exist without exactly one registered cleanup
      transaction ID.
- [x] No inert or no-effect plan appears as active ownership, cleanup history,
      or result data.
- [x] Conflicts and invalid dependency directions are rejected across the
      complete run.

