## Phase 6: Implement cleanup planning and transaction execution

Outcome: executable cleanup transactions have one state machine, deterministic
dependency-safe planning, required verification, and finite execution.

- [x] Add `CleanupPlanner` using temporary
      `CleanupTransactionDependencyGraph` instances backed by the generic DAG.
  - [x] Project active claim edges between their owning transactions.
  - [x] Collapse claim edges within one transaction.
  - [x] Simulate an inert plan as one graph-local prospective transaction node
        before mutation, without allocating or publishing a transaction.
  - [x] Reject claim- or transaction-level cycles during simulation.
  - [x] Revalidate the committed bound subset before linking and before cleanup
        planning.
  - [x] Return dependent-before-prerequisite reverse-topological order.
  - [x] Use reverse owner-local registration order only as the tie-breaker for
        independent transactions.
- [x] Replace bare cleanup callbacks with `CleanupTransaction` handles.
  - [x] Support `pending`, `running`, `complete`, `failed`, `abandoned`, and
        `unconfirmed` state.
  - [x] Make `complete`, `failed`, `abandoned`, and `unconfirmed` the exhaustive
        terminal dispositions.
  - [x] Implement `execute(reason?)`, `isPending()`, and frozen
        `claimReferences()`.
  - [x] Make repeated execution an idempotent no-op returning `false` after an
        already-expended transaction.
  - [x] Remove an eligible transaction from the pending registry before its
        restore callback runs.
  - [x] Keep a dependency-blocked manual transaction pending, publish no start
        event, and allow a later retry after dependents are expended.
  - [x] Expose no public cancellation/discard operation for an effect-backed
        transaction; require manual expenditure or automatic finalization.
- [x] Validate, defensively copy, and freeze cleanup receipts as bounded plain
      data; reject callbacks, userdata, cycles, and live pointers.
- [x] Deliver the same immutable receipt to restore and verification callbacks;
      do not expose mutable transaction state.
- [x] Implement finite cleanup deadlines and fresh cleanup cancellation scopes.
- [x] Make restore one-shot, bounded, and nonblocking.
- [x] Make required cleanup verification read-only and retryable.
  - [x] Accept truthy results and assertion callbacks that return normally.
  - [x] Retry `false`, `cleanup.pending`, and thrown assertion/error observations
        under the remaining cleanup deadline.
  - [x] Fail immediately on `cleanup.fatal`.
  - [x] Attempt verification after restore throws when time remains, while
        preserving the failed transaction outcome.
- [x] Restrict cleanup callbacks.
  - [x] Restore cannot invoke public commands or register cleanup.
  - [x] Verification may invoke only read-only queries and assertions.
  - [x] Mutating commands and cleanup registration are fatal contract errors.
- [x] Implement automatic command, test, suite, and service cleanup through the
      same planner and transaction executor.
- [x] Continue attempting later eligible transactions after an individual
      failure.
- [x] Materialize a prerequisite as `failed/dependency_blocked` when a failed
      or unconfirmed dependent makes safe execution impossible.
- [x] Compose restore, verification, dependency, timeout, and earlier lifecycle
      failures without masking evidence.
- [x] Terminalize cleanup through the claim-disposition policy from Phase 5:
      `complete` only after verified atomic release, and `failed` or
      `unconfirmed` with claims retained for quarantine.
- [x] On manual restore/verification failure, record terminal `failed`, remove
      the transaction from pending work so teardown cannot retry it, then raise
      the composed failure into the currently active Busted lifecycle without
      changing the transaction's tagged owner.
- [x] Add focused transaction/planner tests for every state transition,
      deadline, cancellation, retry, error-composition, LIFO tie-break,
      dependency-blocked, claim release/retention disposition, and callback
      restriction.
- [x] Perform a follow-up cleanup-engine review before integrating it with the
      command runner.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Every cleanup operation has an independent finite deadline and required
      terminal verification or typed abandonment proof.
- [x] The planner never releases a prerequisite ahead of an active dependent.
- [x] Manual and automatic execution use the same state machine and evidence.

Evidence: `Run-UnitTests.ps1 '--filter=Cleanup'` completed with 16 successes;
`Run-UnitTests.ps1 '--filter=ResourceDependencyIndex'` completed with 21
successes; `Check-Lua.ps1`, `Check-SourceDeclarations.ps1`, and `git diff
--check` passed. The required follow-up implementation review and independent
completion audit found no unresolved stage-6 requirement.

## Phase 7: Implement atomic cleanup registration and lifecycle journaling

Outcome: one service atomically creates effect-backed transactions and claims,
and the append-only service journal retains every lifecycle disposition after
pending work is expended.

- [x] Add one run-scoped internal `CleanupRegistrationService` as the only
      mutation boundary for transaction IDs, owner registries, execution
      indexes, receipt freezing, claim linking/release, and lifecycle events.
- [x] Allocate run-unique transaction IDs and owner-local registration ordinals
      before protected state is published.
- [x] Atomically create a transaction and all active claims from a confirmed
      effect receipt and frozen claim bindings.
- [x] Preserve the tagged suite/test owner and owning-command correlation on
      command-lifetime transactions; use lifetime to control finalization, not
      to replace execution-owner identity.
- [x] Revalidate the bound subset against the current resource index at the
      post-effect registration boundary.
- [x] Serialize each DwarfSpec mutating attempt from final plan validation
      through atomic effect registration, including across cooperative yields,
      while allowing nested read-only commands.
- [x] On unexpected post-effect conflict or adapter-contract violation:
  - [x] Preserve the cleanup transaction and receipt.
  - [x] Record fail-closed conflicted ownership evidence.
  - [x] Fail the command at cleanup registration.
