Evidence: `CleanupRegistrationService` tests cover atomic transaction/claim
registration, owner-local ordinals, conflicted post-effect registration,
abandonment, complete/failed/unconfirmed retention, release-failure quarantine,
and terminal-event uniqueness. The focused cleanup suite completed with 28
successes; Lua syntax/formatting passed for 414 files; source declaration
fixtures and `git diff --check` passed. The completion audit subsequently
identified serialization, plan-correlation, and absence-proof gaps; the
resulting corrections and follow-up abandonment authorization fixes have
focused regression coverage. The runner consumes the mutation lease when
command routing is introduced.

## Phase 8: Integrate suite, test, and service cleanup ownership

Outcome: owner identities and cleanup registration are active at every approved
lifecycle level, and each owner closes, finalizes, and reports independently;
failed or unconfirmed cleanup contributes to shared executor quarantine.

This phase establishes the owner identities, registration windows, and cleanup
finalization infrastructure. Phase 10 makes public commands consume that
infrastructure through the common runner and supplies the command-path legality
proof; command routing is not a completion requirement of this phase.

- [x] Introduce stable suite-execution identity for each selected spec file in
      each repeat.
- [x] Activate suite ownership before suite setup and keep it active through
      suite teardown and suite cleanup.
- [x] Activate a nested test-attempt identity before attempt setup and keep it
      active through body, teardown, and attempt cleanup.
- [x] Select the most specific active public owner.
  - [x] Attribute attempt setup/body/teardown work to the test attempt.
  - [x] Attribute suite setup/teardown work outside an attempt to the suite.
  - [x] Reject caller-initiated commands and cleanup registration when neither
        suite nor test ownership is active.
  - [x] Reserve `SERVICE_RUN` commands exclusively for read-only query/assertion
        children of service-owned cleanup verification.
- [x] Support internal service effects through the same transaction abstraction
      and `SERVICE_RUN` cleanup owner.
- [x] Close test registration after attempt teardown, execute remaining pending
      work, terminalize unresolved transactions, and materialize results before
      `test.finished`.
- [x] Close suite registration after suite teardown, execute and terminalize
      remaining suite work, and materialize results before `suite.finished`.
- [x] Guarantee suite finalization after suite setup failure, skipped teardown,
      zero runnable tests, or interruption.
- [x] Run service cleanup after all suite and test owners close and materialize
      it before `run.finished`.
- [x] Apply dependency-safe ordering across owner scopes and retain blocked
      prerequisite evidence when failed/unconfirmed dependents remain.
- [x] Keep manual execution attributed to the transaction's original owner even
      when a suite transaction is executed from a nested test.
- [x] Keep behavior status distinct from cleanup outcome while making failed or
      unconfirmed cleanup affect run failure and quarantine.
- [x] Combine all transaction failures with the existing lifecycle probes before
      setting authoritative `cleanup_confirmed`.
- [x] Ensure emergency finalizers attempt safe pending work and mark only work
      that remains unresolved as `unconfirmed`.
- [x] Add lifecycle tests for ownership selection, setup/teardown legality,
      finalization ordering, setup failure, zero tests, skipped teardown,
      interruption, manual cross-context execution, and service cleanup.
- [x] Perform a follow-up lifecycle review before exposing result projections.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Suite-, test-, and service-owned transactions never leak into another
      owner's registry or result set.
- [x] `test.finished`, `suite.finished`, and `run.finished` are each published
      only after the relevant cleanup projection is terminal.

Evidence recorded 2026-08-08:

- `CleanupOwnerLifecycle` activates deterministic suite and nested test owners,
  finalizes test then suite then service cleanup, and rejects public ownership
  outside an active owner window. `CleanupRegistrationService` closes owner
  registration before executing its pending transactions and retains its
  terminal owner result.
- The Busted suite executor installs the owner windows around file and example
  hooks; run finalization completes any abandoned owner windows before the
  existing cleanup probes compute `cleanup_confirmed`.
- Focused cleanup-service, owner-lifecycle, suite-executor, and assembly tests
