## Phase 16A: Standardize built-in command ownership and registration

Outcome: every verified built-in command is wholly owned by one command-specific
module and installed through one canonical registrar. `ds.lua` constructs
run-scoped capabilities and command instances, then performs one explicit
registration handoff; it does not implement command registration, argument
adaptation, or verified public command functions.

Command-module contract:

- [ ] Add one documented class-like command-module contract for built-ins.
  - [ ] Require exactly one public command definition per module and exactly one
        class-like command owner instance per registered command.
  - [ ] Require `definition()` to return that command's immutable definition and
        `arguments(...)` to translate its public signature into the normalized
        runner request plus trailing command options.
  - [ ] Derive the runner command name from the definition rather than repeating
        a string in the registrar or public closure.
  - [ ] Permit an explicit public binding name only for a non-`ds` surface such
        as a subject method; require the qualified definition name and public
        method name to be declared together by that command module.
  - [ ] Keep normalization, preflight, claims, execution, verification, retry,
        cleanup, workflow, compatibility arguments, and public return mapping in
        the owning command module.
  - [ ] Keep reusable definition builders, immutable/opaque helpers, and runtime
        capability services shared; one command per file must not duplicate
        runner mechanics or domain interaction logic.

Canonical registrar:

- [ ] Add one dependency-injected, class-like `BuiltinCommandRegistrar` that is
      the sole production authority for registering built-in definitions and
      installing their public invocation closures.
  - [ ] Construct it with the verified command runner and the explicit public
        binding surfaces; do not let it locate services, import `ds.lua`, or act
        as a service locator.
  - [ ] Have it validate the command-module interface, obtain and register the
        definition, install a closure that calls the same runner's `invoke()`,
        and return the registered immutable definition.
  - [ ] Reject duplicate definition names, duplicate public names on one
        surface, definition/binding-name mismatches, missing module methods,
        invalid definitions, and replacement of an existing namespace member.
  - [ ] Support explicit `ds` and subject-method binding surfaces without
        inferring targets from dotted strings or silently mutating arbitrary
        tables.
  - [ ] Provide one `register_all()` operation over an explicit ordered array of
        command instances. Do not use filesystem discovery, global registration,
        module-load side effects, or unordered table iteration.
  - [ ] Ensure no built-in command module calls `registerBuiltin()` or assigns a
        verified command directly to `ds`; only the registrar performs those
        mechanics.

One-command module migration:

- [ ] Place registered built-ins under one unambiguous command-module location
      and use one file per public command; do not retain `_definition` aggregates
      as a second ownership layer.
- [ ] Convert the already separated representative commands (`search`, `click`,
      `mountSaveGame`, `stage_overlay_registration`, and `registerCleanup`) to
      the command-module contract and registrar without changing their runtime
      capability boundaries or lifecycle policy.
- [ ] Split `view_position_definition.lua` so `getViewPos` and `setViewPos` have
      independent command owners while continuing to share `MapViewRuntime`.
- [ ] Split `wait_definition.lua` into independent `wait_frames`, `wait_ticks`,
      `await`, and `awaitEvent` command modules while sharing only stateless wait
      option validation and the scheduling capability.
- [ ] Split `game_query_definition.lua` into independent `isGamePaused`,
      `getGameSpeed`, `getTick`, `getTime`, `getSaveDirectoryName`, and `hasFocus`
      command modules.
- [ ] Split `mount_query_definition.lua` into independent `root`, `get`,
      `inspect`, and `capture_view_tree` command modules without duplicating
      mount/source identity logic.
- [ ] Keep `capture_screen` separate from `capture_view_tree`; both may share a
      capture-artifact capability but may not share a command owner.
- [ ] Split `subject_query_definition.lua` into one module per qualified subject
      command and install their method adapters through the registrar's explicit
      subject surface.
- [ ] Move the `registerCleanup` public argument adapter and namespace binding
      out of `ds.lua` and into its owning command module; preserve the privileged
      cleanup capability boundary.
- [ ] Remove every migrated `bind(ds, command_runner, ...)` implementation,
      dynamic `ds[name]` assignment, returned loose command-closure table, and
      direct registration call from command modules.
- [ ] Remove the install-capture-overwrite path for every already verified
      command. `ds.lua` must not first install a legacy implementation merely so
      a verified command can capture it; inject the underlying cohesive runtime
      capability directly instead.
- [ ] Remove the `command_waits`, `scalar_queries`, anonymous mount-query
      operations, and equivalent loose callback bundles rather than moving them
      unchanged into per-command constructors.
  - [ ] Reuse or introduce narrow class-like scheduling, game-query, run-query,
        capture, and mount-query capabilities where mutable run state or host
        ownership is involved.
  - [ ] Pass an imported function/module directly only when it is demonstrably
        stateless and dependency-free under the Phase 15A classification rule.

Composition and dependency direction:

- [ ] Make `ds.lua` explicitly construct cohesive runtime capabilities and the
      ordered array of command owner instances, then call the registrar once.
- [ ] Keep command ordering explicit and deterministic while avoiding a second
      command catalog that duplicates names, definitions, or binding metadata.
- [ ] Preserve the dependency direction `ds.lua -> command modules -> runtime
      capabilities/shared command helpers`; command modules and the registrar
      must not import host/controller layers or the assembled `ds` facade.
- [ ] Distinguish still-unconverted legacy public functions from registrar-owned
      verified commands in the migration inventory so later work cannot treat a
      temporary direct binding as the canonical pattern.

Verification and review:

- [ ] Add focused registrar tests for successful ordered registration, exact
      public argument forwarding, return preservation, duplicate rejection,
      surface mismatch, namespace replacement, invalid module contracts, and
      use of the exact injected runner.
- [ ] Give every split command module its own focused suite; retain shared
      conformance tests for cross-command lifecycle behavior rather than an
      aggregate module-specific suite.
- [ ] Add an inventory/source-organization test proving every already verified
      built-in has exactly one command module, one registered definition, and
      one public binding, and that no migrated module bypasses the registrar.
- [ ] Update package/source declarations for all split modules and remove the
      obsolete aggregate module paths in the same change.
- [ ] Rerun all affected representative and read-only command suites, registrar
      and shared conformance suites, runner/public-binding/host-assembly suites,
      the full unit suite, Lua formatting, source declarations, and dependency
      checks.
- [ ] Run the smallest current-worktree native qualification covering a query,
      wait/assertion, capture, subject method, representative mutator/workflow,
      and terminal cleanup confirmation after registration changes.
- [ ] Perform the required follow-up implementation review and an independent
      completion audit before treating the registrar contract as the basis for
      Phases 17-21.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every already verified built-in command has one class-like command owner
      in one command-specific file; no aggregate owns multiple public commands.
- [ ] One registrar exclusively registers built-in definitions and installs
      verified `ds` and subject-method bindings, with deterministic explicit
      composition and no discovery or service locator.
- [ ] `ds.lua` contains capability and command construction plus one registrar
      handoff, with no verified-command definition, argument adapter, direct
      registration, or public invocation closure.
- [ ] Phase 15 representative and Phase 16 read-only observable behavior remains
      unchanged under refreshed focused, full, static, declaration, dependency,
      and cleanup-confirmed live evidence.

Conversion invariant for Phases 17-21:

- Every newly converted built-in uses the Phase 16A command-module contract and
  canonical registrar immediately. A family aggregate, command-local
  `bind(ds, ...)`, direct `registerBuiltin()`, direct verified `ds` assignment,
  or install-capture-overwrite adapter is a conversion defect, not temporary
  scaffolding to defer to Phase 21.

## Phase 17: Convert game and simulation state setters

Outcome: reversible game/simulation mutations execute once, verify exact
framework-owned state, and retain independently verified restoration.

- [ ] Convert `setGamePaused`, `setGameSpeed`, and `setTurboSpeed` with native
      readiness and exact requested readback.
- [ ] Implement each converted state setter as its own command module and
      install it only through the canonical registrar.
- [ ] Complete `setViewPos` conversion and all origin/viewport edge cases.
- [ ] Convert `setUnitPos` with unit/source/destination occupancy preflight and
      receipt-based unit/occupancy verification.
- [ ] Convert `setUnitSpeed` with immutable target/configuration snapshots,
      recurring-operation ownership verification, and optional caller
      verification for product progress.
- [ ] Convert any other inventoried state-setter command.
- [ ] Capture inherited baselines before mutation and register owner-lifetime
      cleanup immediately after each confirmed effect.
- [ ] Keep all initial mutators `ONCE`.
- [ ] Add cleanup receipts containing stable identities/scalar baselines and
      independent retryable cleanup verification.
- [ ] Add conformance fixtures and focused domain tests for no-effect,
      structured partial effect, verification failure, command expiry, manual
      cleanup, automatic cleanup, and composed failure.
- [ ] Run the smallest relevant native live case for each changed semantic
      boundary.
- [ ] At family completion, run the full unit suite and representative live
      qualification with terminal cleanup confirmation.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up family review against the migration inventory.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every state setter proves its exact declared state or raises a staged test
      error while retaining cleanup ownership.
- [ ] Cleanup verifies restoration independently of the command deadline.

## Phase 18: Convert pointer, keyboard, text, mouse, and click actions

Outcome: generic interactions stop equating non-throwing dispatch/render with
product success and instead expose the strongest truthful framework guarantee.

- [ ] Complete `move_pointer` and `hover` conversion with current geometry,
      actionability, logical accessor, and paired raw-coordinate verification.
- [ ] Implement each converted interaction as its own command module and install
      it only through the canonical registrar.
- [ ] Convert `input` and `type` with current ingress preflight, explicit
      dispatch acknowledgement, and required settling/render verification.
- [ ] Convert `mouseInput`, `mouseWheel`, and remaining `click` paths with
      target, pointer, button, geometry, transient-state restoration, and render
      guarantees.
- [ ] Keep all interaction mutations `ONCE`.
- [ ] Do not claim input consumption unless the selected ingress provides a
      reliable consumption signal.
- [ ] Preserve existing public wait-by-default behavior and retain `{wait=false}`
      only for signatures that explicitly promise asynchronous invalidation.
- [ ] Use internal operations for composite pointer placement and dispatch;
      never invoke a second public mutating command from primary execution.
- [ ] Recommend optional caller verification for intended product behavior in
      declarations, examples, and conformance cases.
- [ ] Add focused cases where input is ignored/unhandled or reaches an
      unexpected screen so intrinsic success is not mislabeled as product
      success.
- [ ] Run focused suites during mechanical conversion and the smallest live
      case for changed native ingress/geometry/render behavior.
- [ ] At family completion, run the full unit suite and representative live
      action qualification with terminal cleanup confirmation.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up family review against the migration inventory.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every interaction command has explicit actionability and intrinsic
      evidence boundaries.
- [ ] Generic action success never implies an unobservable product outcome.

## Phase 19: Convert mount, redraw, viewport, and capture ownership paths

Outcome: screen/mount/render commands use the common workflow, state,
verification, deadline, and cleanup contracts without legacy mutation wrappers.

- [ ] Convert `mount` and `mountNativeScreen` as workflows with source,
      no-current-mount, ownership, target, subject-source, and render-capability
      checks.
- [ ] Implement each converted mount/render command as its own command module
      and install it only through the canonical registrar.
- [ ] Convert `unmount` as a workflow verifying no current mount, owned active
      screen, retained subject, or borrowed attachment remains.
- [ ] Convert `redraw` with actionability and later completed render generation
      when waiting is enabled.
- [ ] Convert `viewport` as a state setter restricted to a DwarfSpec-owned
      resizable host, with applied dimensions/layout and completed render
      verification.
- [ ] Complete capture command integration and artifact-result formatting.
- [ ] Reuse existing mount adapters and ownership services as domain capability
      providers without allowing them to publish command success directly.
- [ ] Replace mounted-mutation wrapper cleanup/readback with receipts and common
      runner lifecycle where applicable.
- [ ] Preserve borrowed/native-screen ownership and cleanup semantics.
- [ ] Add conformance fixtures and focused mount/render/capture tests.
- [ ] Run focused native live cases where target resolution, rendering, mount
      ownership, cleanup, or scheduler boundaries changed.
- [ ] At family completion, run the full unit suite and representative live
      qualification with terminal cleanup confirmation.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up family review before removing obsolete wrappers.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Mount/render/capture commands have no parallel success or cleanup path.
- [ ] Existing supported public returns, subject behavior, and asynchronous
      redraw option semantics remain compatible.

## Phase 20: Convert save workflows and resource-producing fixtures

Outcome: long workflows and fixture commands use named verified steps,
effect-driven claims, mandatory cleanup verification, and shared reporting.

- [ ] Convert `exitToMainMenu` and complete `mountSaveGame`/related save-load
      workflow conversion with exact intermediate and terminal native states.
- [ ] Implement each converted workflow or fixture as its own command module
