- [ ] Convert overlay registration fixtures through the common fixture
      definition, claims, effect receipt, and cleanup transaction model.
- [ ] Reconcile every implemented managed native-fixture command with
      `docs/new-command-proposals.md`.
  - [ ] Keep `reserveMapTiles` as a real effect-producing domain command rather
        than a generic pre-effect reservation handle.
  - [ ] Apply shared `registerCleanup`, timeout, retry, receipt, claims,
        dependency, journal, and validation contracts.
  - [ ] Do not implement a fixture-specific runner, cleanup registry, graph,
        ledger, or validation cadence.
- [ ] Ensure each fixture validates an inert conservative claim plan before
      mutation and atomically activates only effect-bound claims afterward.
- [ ] Ensure adapters return structured partial-effect receipts for any native
      operation that can mutate before failure.
- [ ] Keep initial fixture mutations `ONCE`.
- [ ] Add focused workflow/fixture/domain tests and shared conformance fixtures.
- [ ] Add live proof for exact creation/transition, stable identity, ownership,
      dependency ordering, and verified absence/restoration.
- [ ] At family completion, run the full unit suite and representative live
      qualification with terminal cleanup confirmation.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up reconciliation review across both proposals.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every save/fixture effect is registered before verification or
      publication and can be recovered from its immutable receipt.
- [ ] Managed fixtures consume the shared architecture without duplicating it.

## Phase 21: Complete conversion and remove compatibility scaffolding

Outcome: one definition-based runner and one canonical built-in registrar own
the entire command surface, and all temporary execution/configuration paths are
removed at an explicit verified checkpoint.

- [ ] Reconcile the migration inventory and convert every remaining built-in,
      subject, project, repository-fixture, and internal public command path.
- [ ] Reconcile the canonical registrar inventory one-for-one against every
      built-in definition and public binding surface; reject missing, duplicate,
      aggregate-owned, or bypassed commands.
- [ ] Make `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua` a thin composition facade that constructs
      capabilities and command owners and performs one registrar handoff, with
      no duplicate pointer, input, game, mount, workflow, timeout, verification,
      cleanup, argument-adaptation, or namespace-installation implementation.
- [ ] Remove old direct command binding and execution paths after all callers
      route through the registry and runner.
- [ ] Remove every remaining command-local `bind(ds, ...)`, direct built-in
      `registerBuiltin()`, verified `function ds.*`/`ds[name]` assignment,
      returned loose command table, and install-capture-overwrite adapter.
- [ ] Remove obsolete mounted-mutation wrappers only after their consumers and
      ownership tests have migrated.
- [ ] Remove any temporary migration-only adapters, switches, duplicated event
      translation, or compatibility fixtures.
- [ ] End timeout compatibility.
  - [ ] Reject `timeout_ms=false` in finalized configuration.
  - [ ] Stop using `settings.wait.timeout_ms` as a command default.
  - [ ] Remove bounded deprecation diagnostics and compatibility-only tests.
- [ ] Confirm project definitions are the exclusive custom-command format and
      bare callbacks fail during loading.
- [ ] Confirm every public command resolves a finite timeout, has explicit
      preflight, selects intrinsic evidence, declares mutation/retry behavior,
      and preserves its approved public return.
- [ ] Run dependency/source-organization checks proving driver modules do not
      import host internals and no duplicate facade implementation remains.
- [ ] Run registrar/source-organization checks proving every built-in has one
      owning module and only the registrar installs verified public bindings.
- [ ] Run the full unit suite and representative live qualification at this
      destructive removal checkpoint.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up removal review using searches for every old API,
      wrapper, setting fallback, callback shape, and direct-binding pattern.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every public command reaches exactly one run-scoped runner.
- [ ] No legacy adapter, parallel command implementation, or unlimited command
      timeout remains.
- [ ] Every built-in command has exactly one module, definition, registrar entry,
      and public binding; no aggregate or direct namespace-registration path
      remains.

## Phase 22: Update documentation, package contracts, and release guidance

Outcome: public documentation accurately describes verified guarantees,
optional caller semantics, project command conversion, cleanup receipts, and
the breaking protocol/configuration changes.

